<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1980">1980</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as FMLN</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti">Farabundo Martí</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> (FMLN) was formed in October 1980 as a coalition of five Salvadoran left-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> organisations and named after the communist leader Farabundo Martí. It fought the government in the civil war of 1980–1992, which ended with the Chapultepec Peace Accords. It then became a legal political party and held the presidency from 2009 to 2019.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front.jpg" alt="Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ivan Ch RU · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_of_the_FMLN.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Communist Party of El Salvador (PCES) was founded in 1930. In 1970 its general secretary Cayetano Carpio left it, convinced that elections could not transform the country, and founded the Farabundo Martí Popular Liberation Forces (FPL), calling for a prolonged popular war against the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> that had ruled since 1931.</p><p>Other groups followed, among them the People's Revolutionary Army, the National Resistance Armed Forces and, in 1979, the Armed Liberation Forces of the PCES. The FMLN was established on 10 October 1980 by the merger of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups, the PRTC joining in December, and in 1981 it allied with the Revolutionary Democratic Front.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The FMLN launched its first major offensive on 10 January 1981 with 43 attacks. It held 82 towns and villages by 17 January, but the government's counteroffensive prevailed and by 26 January the offensive had failed to spark the national uprising it sought.</p><p>A second offensive on 28 March 1982 failed to stop the election of a Constitutional Assembly. In 1983 Carpio, who rejected negotiation, had his rival Mélida Anaya Montes killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a> and took his own life days later. The war ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-peace-accords">Chapultepec Peace Accords</a>, under which the FMLN demobilised.</p><p>The Legislative Assembly legalised the FMLN on 30 June 1992 and it was registered as a party on 14 December. In the first postwar election, in 1994, its candidate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a> reached the second round and lost to ARENA's Armando Calderón Sol with 31.7 per cent, and the party became the second largest in the Assembly. For about three decades it shared the national stage with the right-wing ARENA.</p><p>It lost its first three presidential elections, then won in 2009 with the journalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauricio-funes">Mauricio Funes</a> and in 2014 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-sanchez-ceren">Salvador Sánchez Cerén</a>, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> commander. It lost the next two, and after the 2024 election it held no seats in the Legislative Assembly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the civil war the member groups ranged from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism-Leninism</a> and a Maoist current to social democratic positions, and the front was inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution">Cuban Revolution</a> and liberation theology. Between 1988 and 1991 it abandoned Marxism-Leninism and embraced democratic socialism. Catholic clergy had a prominent part, and some priests became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandinista-national-liberation-front">Sandinista National Liberation Front</a>, which took power in Nicaragua in 1979, was a source of inspiration and support. The party has belonged to the São Paulo Forum, to COPPPAL and to the Parliamentary Group of the Left in the Central American Parliament, and several splinter parties have broken from it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The civil war of 1980–1992 pitted a US-funded army, expanded to several times its prewar size, against the <b>Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front</b> (FMLN), formed in 1980 from five guerrilla organisations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Military</a></li></ul></section>
